    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.metric_closure - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.metric_closure.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.metric_closure()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Build the metric closure: a complete graph of shortest-path distances.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.metric_closure()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Build the metric closure: a complete graph of shortest-path distances.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.metric_closure()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: >-\n  Build the metric closure: a complete graph of shortest-path distances.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nBuild the metric closure: a complete graph of shortest-path distances.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L77838-L77974){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.metric_closure(weighted=None)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Metric closure](../assets/graphs/metric-closure.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Left: a path A, B, C, D whose three edges each have weight 1. Right: the metric closure joins every pair of nodes, weighted by the shortest-path distance between them. It keeps the three unit edges and adds the accent shortcuts A–C with weight 2, B–D with weight 2, and A–D with weight 3.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nThe metric closure has the same nodes as the graph and an edge between every pair of\nnodes, weighted by the length of the shortest path between them. It turns any connected\nnetwork into a complete \"distance table\" that satisfies the triangle inequality, which\nis what many approximation algorithms expect: traveling salesperson heuristics (see\n`traveling_salesman()`), Steiner tree approximations, and clustering or facility\nlocation on network distances.\n\nOn a directed graph, the result is a complete directed graph whose edge from `u` to `v`\nholds the distance from `u` to `v`.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[weighted]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Whether to use edge weights as lengths. If `None` (default), follows the graph's\n    `weighted` flag; with `False` the distances are hop counts. Weights must be\n    non-negative.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   A new weighted graph with the same directedness and nodes (IDs, labels and\n    attributes kept), with one edge per pair of nodes (per ordered pair if directed)\n    weighted by distance.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If the graph is not connected (strongly connected if directed), or a weight is\n    negative.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nDijkstra's algorithm runs from every node, for $O(V (E + V) \\log V)$ time, and the\nresult has $V (V - 1) / 2$ edges (twice as many if directed), so it is meant for small\nand medium graphs. Unlike NetworkX, the edges do not store the shortest paths\nthemselves; use `shortest_path()` to recover one.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~traveling_salesman`) \n\n:   Plan a round trip on the closure.\n\n[](`~all_pairs_shortest_path_length`) \n\n:   The same distances as a nested dict.\n\n[](`~steiner_tree`) \n\n:   Another approximation built on the closure.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nThe closure of a path joins every pair of nodes, weighted by the distance along the path:\n\n::: {#ace9f381 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\npath = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"B\", 1.0), (\"B\", \"C\", 2.0), (\"C\", \"D\", 1.0)], weighted=True\n)\n\npath.metric_closure().get_edge_df(labels=True).select(\n    \"source\", \"target\", \"weight\"\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```{=html}\n<div><style>\n.dataframe > thead > tr,\n.dataframe > tbody > tr {\n  text-align: right;\n  white-space: pre-wrap;\n}\n</style>\n<small>shape: (6, 3)</small><table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\"><thead><tr><th>source</th><th>target</th><th>weight</th></tr><tr><td>str</td><td>str</td><td>f64</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&quot;A&quot;</td><td>&quot;B&quot;</td><td>1.0</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;A&quot;</td><td>&quot;C&quot;</td><td>3.0</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;A&quot;</td><td>&quot;D&quot;</td><td>4.0</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;B&quot;</td><td>&quot;C&quot;</td><td>2.0</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;B&quot;</td><td>&quot;D&quot;</td><td>3.0</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;C&quot;</td><td>&quot;D&quot;</td><td>1.0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### A mileage chart\n\n`towns()` holds 12 towns of a made-up county and the 26 roads between them. Like the\nmileage chart of a road atlas, the closure gives the distance by road between every pair\nof towns, 66 in all. The farthest apart are Ashby and Millbrook, at opposite corners of\nthe county:\n\n::: {#ee04bd32 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport polars as pl\n\ntowns = ec.towns()\nclosure = towns.metric_closure()\n\n(\n    closure.get_edge_df(labels=True)\n    .select(\"source\", \"target\", km=pl.col(\"weight\"))\n    .sort(\"km\", descending=True)\n    .head(3)\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```{=html}\n<div><style>\n.dataframe > thead > tr,\n.dataframe > tbody > tr {\n  text-align: right;\n  white-space: pre-wrap;\n}\n</style>\n<small>shape: (3, 3)</small><table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\"><thead><tr><th>source</th><th>target</th><th>km</th></tr><tr><td>str</td><td>str</td><td>f64</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&quot;Ashby&quot;</td><td>&quot;Millbrook&quot;</td><td>133.0</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Ashby&quot;</td><td>&quot;Larkhill&quot;</td><td>124.0</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Ashby&quot;</td><td>&quot;Norbury&quot;</td><td>117.0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nDividing each distance by the straight line between the towns shows the detours. The road\nfrom Bramford to Elmwick is 38% longer than the straight line, the most of any pair: no\nroad joins them, and the shortest route goes by way of Calder:\n\n::: {#0c4355bc .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport math\n\npos = closure.get_node_attrs(\"pos\")\nkm = closure.get_edge_attrs(\"weight\")\nends = closure.get_node_ids_by_edge_id\n\ndef detour(edge):\n    u, v = ends(edge)\n    return km[edge] / math.dist(pos[u], pos[v])\n\nworst = max(km, key=detour)\npair = [closure.get_label_from_id(n) for n in ends(worst)]\n\npair, round(detour(worst), 2)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n(['Bramford', 'Elmwick'], 1.38)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#83452deb .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ntowns.shortest_path(\"Bramford\", \"Elmwick\").plot(\n    layout=\"pos\", highlight=\"shortest_path_order\", label_by=\"label\"\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-63cc1385:#3f3f46;--ect-879b7ebc:#2c2c31;--ect-b9f8de6d:#ae500f;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-63cc1385:#3f3f46;--ect-879b7ebc:#2c2c31;--ect-b9f8de6d:#ae500f;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}</style><div class=\"ec-plot\" style=\"position:relative;width:fit-content;max-width:100%;margin:1em auto\">\n<style>.ec-plot > svg { display:block; max-width:100%; height: